1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:25,273
Nigga, that's a hurricane. Tank stood under the hotel canopy watching
rain bounce off the pavement. It's a storm, Lark said. It just threw a
chair. A terrace chair skated past us and hit a planter. Mateo caught
it before it reached the road. See? Tank said. Nigga trying to furnish
the harbor. Mateo laughed, carried the chair inside and came back to
Lark.

2
00:00:25,523 --> 00:00:46,798
Three days free. Already he knew Tank would hold weather personally
responsible for its behavior. The Night Baptism was supposed to start
at sunset. Nobody had seen the sun for hours, so an official announced
that it had probably set and carried on with his flags. Rain lashed
the harbor. Boats pulled at their ropes.

3
00:00:47,048 --> 00:01:11,315
The course ran along the coast, through tunnels and over a causeway to
a lighthouse, then back toward the city. It should have been
postponed. Aurelio offered each driver the chance to withdraw, taking
care to stand where the camera could record every refusal. Nigga
brought a photographer to a forecast. I chose the dark green Aston
Martin from the loan fleet.

4
00:01:11,565 --> 00:01:34,410
The police Lamborghini needed a rest, according to Lark, who had
explained this with the keys in her pocket. She put one hand on the
Aston's roof. Bring this one back. Nigga, I bring things back.
Assembled. Mateo bent over the open door to check a fitting. That does
make a difference. You been free three days and already joining her
side?

5
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:56,513
Nigga, I picked her side before they locked me up. Lark handed him a
tool without looking. He handed another back. They disagreed about
something beneath the dashboard, kissed briefly when they both
straightened, and went right back to disagreeing. I watched until Lark
caught me. What? Nothing. Car looks good. Then look at the car.

6
00:01:56,763 --> 00:02:18,321
Shawna arrived in a dark coat over her team jacket, carrying my
helmet. Water shone in her hair. She made me look at her before she
handed it over. You don't have to prove the storm wrong. I ain't mad
at the storm. Good. Nigga ain't met me. She closed her eyes, then
laughed despite herself. There he is. I took the helmet.

7
00:02:18,571 --> 00:02:39,829
She kept hold of it one moment longer. Come back and tell me about it.
I kissed her forehead. Every turn. Nero stood beside Cassius's silver
car at the grid, speaking quietly to his son. Inez waited nearby in
red, watching the rain strike the road. Rafe had withdrawn. Adrian had
entered.

8
00:02:40,079 --> 00:03:03,204
So had six other drivers whose pride, ambition or bank accounts had
made a complicated decision on their behalf. The duke stood beneath a
huge green umbrella. The stranger in the dark uniform stood behind him
without one. Rain ran off his shoulders. Nigga didn't blink. I walked
past both of them toward the car. Aurelio spoke.

9
00:03:03,454 --> 00:03:27,643
Perhaps tonight we discover whether you are fortunate or talented.
Nigga, you discovered talent at the fountain. Now you're investigating
denial. The mechanics nearby burst out laughing. He let the sound pass
over him. The sea gives nothing back for admiration. Then stop
watching me so hard. I got in. The light changed. We left into the
rain.

10
00:03:27,893 --> 00:03:53,320
The Aston moved differently from the Lamborghini, heavier through the
first turn, planted when the road opened. I kept it steady while water
blurred the windshield and headlights tore long tunnels through the
darkness ahead. Adrian took an early lead. Inez followed him. Cassius
stayed just behind me, patient, listening to the road instead of the
audience. The city vanished behind us.

11
00:03:53,570 --> 00:04:17,755
On the coast road, the wind struck sideways hard enough to move the
whole car. I corrected and felt the tires answer. Rain drummed against
the roof. The engine held its deep note beneath everything, a sound I
could trust when the world outside had become water and occasional
stone. Nigga, I told the car, we doing fine. It did not answer in
words.

12
00:04:18,005 --> 00:04:40,992
Cars know when to be professional. We reached the first tunnel in
fourth. Inside, the sudden absence of rain felt almost violent. The
road shone under the lamps. I passed Adrian's teammate at the exit,
came out into the storm in third and saw Inez's red taillights ahead.
She moved left before the next bend. I stayed right.

13
00:04:41,242 --> 00:04:58,592
We ran side by side through a wall of spray. Then her voice came over
the channel. You enjoying the ocean now? Nigga, my wife wanted all of
it. She laughed, and we took the curve together. At the lighthouse
causeway, the marshals waved yellow flags.

14
00:04:58,842 --> 00:05:21,651
Water washed over the low wall in bursts, not deep enough to stop the
cars but enough to make every movement matter. Adrian slowed. Inez
held back. I came alongside the white car at the first long straight
and saw his face through the window, jaw tight, eyes fixed ahead. He
was frightened. Good drivers get frightened.

15
00:05:21,901 --> 00:05:44,643
They just don't always let the fear choose their hands. I passed him
cleanly. The lighthouse rose ahead, its beam sweeping the rain. We
circled beneath it and turned back toward the mainland. The sea hit
the wall with a force that made the road shake. Then the lights went
out again. Not the harbor this time. The lighthouse. The beam stopped.

16
00:05:44,893 --> 00:06:06,971
For one second the whole causeway disappeared. A truck came out of the
darkness at the far end, headlights high, moving toward the race
against the traffic. Nigga. I saw the shape. The distance. The narrow
space between its wheels and the wall. I didn't have time to be angry.
I took the Aston toward the low sea barrier.

17
00:06:07,221 --> 00:06:28,924
The right wheels climbed the stone edge. The car tilted. Rain struck
the side window. For a breath we ran on two wheels, the truck filling
the space where my hood should have been. Its mirror struck mine.
Glass vanished. Then I was past. The Aston came down hard, rear tire
bursting as it hit the road.

18
00:06:29,174 --> 00:06:51,494
I fought the wheel, kept the nose straight and heard the truck brake
behind me as Inez and Adrian scattered around it. Truck on the
causeway! I shouted into the channel. Cassius answered. I see it. A
crash sounded behind me. Then his voice again. Clear. We're clear. I
breathed. The tire was gone. The car dragged to the right.

19
00:06:51,744 --> 00:07:14,231
Sparks ran beneath the rear corner, bright against the rain. Ahead lay
five miles to the city and the finish. Lark's voice came through.
Aiden, stop. The wheel still on. Nigga, that isn't the whole
requirement. I can feel it. Then feel where you can pull over. I
slowed enough to bring the car under control. Other drivers passed.

20
00:07:14,481 --> 00:07:39,021
Inez first, then Adrian, then Cassius, who stayed beside me for a
moment. You need me? he called. Nigga, go win something. He hesitated.
Go! He went. I found a marshal's shelter near the mainland end of the
causeway. Two officials stood beneath it, white-faced. One had a radio
that wasn't working. The other stared at my ruined wheel. Do you have
a service car?

21
00:07:39,271 --> 00:08:01,076
I asked. He nodded toward an old blue coupe parked beneath a tree. It
was not a racing car. It looked like a schoolteacher had lent it to
his cousin and expected it back with a full tank. The rules allowed a
replacement vehicle in the event of race interference, provided the
driver completed the course from the point of the stop.

22
00:08:01,326 --> 00:08:24,836
Nero had made me read that part before the start. Nigga believed in
knowing where a duke might try to lie. I pointed at the coupe. That
one. The official shook his head. It's mine. You got insurance? Yes,
but— I got money. We both protected. He looked at my face. Then at the
race disappearing into the rain. Then he handed me the keys.

23
00:08:25,086 --> 00:08:44,912
My wife named it Clementine. Nigga, Clementine about to become famous.
The marshal looked at his keys in my hand. "Nigga, you really taking
Clementine?" "Nigga, she ready." "She's never been in a race."
"Neither had that nigga's boat yesterday. Look what happened." He
stared toward the storm.

24
00:08:45,162 --> 00:09:07,164
"My wife going to kill me." "Tell her the nigga with the badge
borrowed it." "She'll say which nigga." "Then show her the race." He
laughed, frightened and delighted at once. "Nigga, bring my eggs." "I
heard you the first time, breakfast captain." I got in. The seat
smelled faintly of peppermint. A little knitted bird hung from the
mirror.

25
00:09:07,414 --> 00:09:25,794
There was a bag of groceries in the back and a classical music station
playing softly beneath the storm. I moved the eggs onto the floor.
Greatness respects breakfast. Then I drove. Clementine did not leap
forward. Clementine considered. Then she went.

26
00:09:26,044 --> 00:09:47,568
I took the first bend clean, let the little car gather speed on the
descent and felt its narrow body slip through the wind more easily
than the heavier Aston had. It had no business being in the race.
Neither had I, according to most of the people whose names were now
beneath mine on the standings. We understood each other.

27
00:09:47,818 --> 00:10:09,663
At the old tunnel I caught the last racing car. The driver looked
over. Looked again. Nigga, I said through the open window, you getting
passed by groceries. I went around him. The radio crackled with
disbelief from the commentary booth. They had found me on a camera and
were trying to decide whether the picture had been mislabeled.

28
00:10:09,913 --> 00:10:31,876
Captain Aiden appears to be driving— A car, Tank shouted somewhere
behind the open team channel. Nigga, finish the sentence. Lark was
laughing now. She didn't want to. I could hear her fighting it. Keep
it together, she told me. Me or Clementine? Both of you fools. We
climbed toward the city. One car. Then another.

29
00:10:32,126 --> 00:10:54,502
The little blue coupe ran beneath the lamps with its knitted bird
swinging wildly and the eggs safe on the floor. I drove every turn as
if I had known the machine my whole life. No blue thunder. No borrowed
power. Just hands, road and a stubborn little engine discovering it
had more in it than its owner had ever asked.

30
00:10:54,752 --> 00:11:18,852
By the harbor I was fourth. The crowd saw me. Nigga, I will never
forget that sound. It started with laughter. Not mockery. Astonishment
too large to come out politely. Then it became shouting. People
pointed at the blue coupe, at my badge held against the windshield, at
the grocery bag visible through the rear window. That's him! Nigga,
that's Aiden!

31
00:11:19,102 --> 00:11:42,180
He in a regular car! The nigga made it irregular! Inez saw me in her
mirror and started laughing over the radio. No. Yes. Aiden. Nigga,
move. I got milk. She fought me through the next bend anyway. Nearly
held me. I passed her at the exit with the knitted bird swinging like
it was cheering for its own family. Cassius was second. Adrian first.

32
00:11:42,430 --> 00:12:04,738
The final straight opened before us, wet and bright beneath the city
lights. Adrian's boat-race pride had become a car-race lead, and he
meant to keep it. Cassius pressed him on the inside. I came through
the gap between their spray. Clementine's engine screamed. The little
bird hit the windshield. We crossed first by half a bumper.

33
00:12:04,988 --> 00:12:27,963
I stopped beneath the finish arch with steam rising from the hood and
the classical station still playing. For three seconds nobody moved.
Then the official whose car I had borrowed arrived on the back of a
service motorcycle and fell to his knees in front of Clementine. My
wife is never going to believe this. I got out and handed him the
eggs.

34
00:12:28,213 --> 00:12:50,192
Nigga, breakfast intact. Shawna ran toward me laughing so hard she
could barely breathe. She hit my chest with both hands, then kissed
me, rain on her face, joy everywhere. You won in that? I looked back
at the little blue coupe. Baby, that's Clementine. The crowd took up
the name. Clementine. Clementine. Clementine.

35
00:12:50,442 --> 00:13:03,157
The duke presented the fifth key while a thousand people chanted for a
schoolteacher's grocery car. Nigga, I had never seen a rich man lose
to produce before. It looked excellent on him.
